        "explanation": "React Fiber is the core rendering engine architecture of React (introduced in React 16). \nBefore Fiber, React used a synchronous 'Stack' reconciler. If a massive UI update happened, React would block the main thread until the entire tree was updated (causing dropped frames and stuttering animations). \nFiber rewrote the engine to allow **Asynchronous, Interruptible Rendering**. It breaks rendering work into small chunks ('fibers'), allowing React to pause rendering, let the browser handle high-priority tasks (like user typing or animations), and then resume rendering.",
