        "category": "React Hooks",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Hooks were introduced to solve three massive problems with older React Class components:\n1. **Giant Components:** Lifecycle methods (`componentDidMount`, etc.) forced unrelated logic (like fetching data AND setting up event listeners) to be smashed together into one block of code.\n2. **Wrapper Hell:** Sharing logic between classes required messy patterns like Higher-Order Components (HOCs) or Render Props, resulting in deeply nested, unreadable DOM trees.\n3. **Classes are Confusing:** The `this` keyword in JS is famously tricky, requiring manual `.bind(this)` on methods, and classes minimize poorly during production builds.",
        "example": "With Custom Hooks, you can extract logic out of a component. You can write a `useWindowSize()` hook and share it across 50 components instantly.",
        "code": "// Pre-Hooks: Sharing logic required \"Wrapper Hell\"\n<WindowSizeListener>\n    {(size) => (\n        <ThemeContext.Consumer>\n            {(theme) => <MyComponent size={size} theme={theme} />}\n        </ThemeContext.Consumer>\n    )}\n</WindowSizeListener>\n\n// Post-Hooks: Flat, incredibly readable logic\nfunction MyComponent() {\n    const size = useWindowSize();\n    const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);\n    return <div>...</div>;\n}",
        "tip": "Interviewers love this question. Focus on 'Logic Extraction/Reusability' and 'Solving Wrapper Hell'.",
        "mistake": "Saying they were introduced to make React faster. Hooks do not offer a performance improvement; they offer a massive Developer Experience (DX) and code organization improvement.",
