        "category": "React Hooks",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "React relies on strict rules to ensure Hooks work correctly under the hood:\n1. **Only call Hooks at the Top Level:** Never call hooks inside loops, conditions, or nested functions. React relies on the exact order in which hooks are called to associate them with the correct state. If a condition skips a hook, the order breaks, and the app crashes.\n2. **Only call Hooks from React Functions:** You can only call hooks from React Functional Components, or from your own Custom Hooks. Never call them from standard vanilla JS functions.",
        "example": "If you have an `if` statement, put it INSIDE the `useEffect`, do not put the `useEffect` inside the `if` statement.",
