        "explanation": "Internally, React does not magically 'know' which `useState` belongs to which variable. \nReact manages hooks as a **linked list (or array)** stored on the Fiber node of that specific component. \nWhen a component renders, React executes the hooks in the exact order they are written. The 1st `useState` call points to index 0 of the hidden array, the 2nd to index 1, etc. This is EXACTLY why you cannot put hooks in `if` statements—if a hook is skipped, all subsequent hooks read from the wrong index, causing massive bugs.",
