        "category": "State Management & Redux",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "State management is the process of tracking, updating, and sharing data across a React application. \n- **Local State:** Data specific to one component (e.g., is a dropdown open?). Handled by `useState`.\n- **Global State:** Data required by multiple independent components across the app (e.g., Shopping Cart items, User Authentication status, UI Theme). Handled by Context API, Redux, Zustand, or MobX.",
        "example": "If a user adds an item to their cart on the `/product` page, the cart icon in the top Navbar (a completely separate component) needs to update from 0 to 1 immediately. State management facilitates this communication.",
        "code": "// Local State (useState) -> Easy, confined.\n// Global State (Redux/Context) -> Shared everywhere.",
        "tip": "The golden rule of React state: 'Keep state as local as possible, and only elevate it to global state when absolutely necessary.'",
        "mistake": "Putting every single variable in the entire app into a Redux global store. This makes the app incredibly slow and difficult to maintain.",
        "summary": "The architectural practice of handling data changes in an application, specifically determining how to share 'global' data seamlessly between disconnected components."
