        "id": 82,
        "title": "What is Redux?",
        "category": "State Management & Redux",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Redux is a predictable state container for JavaScript apps. \nIt acts as a single, centralized global store for an application's entire state. Components can read data from this store, and update the store by 'dispatching actions'. Because it enforces strict rules on how state can be updated, it makes large-scale applications highly predictable and easy to debug (via Time-Travel debugging).",
        "example": "Used extensively in massive enterprise apps where data flow gets too chaotic for Context API or prop drilling.",
        "code": "// In Redux, state is completely separate from the UI components.\n// Store -> UI -> Action -> Reducer -> Store (Strict one-way loop)",
        "tip": "Redux is framework-agnostic (can be used with Angular or Vanilla JS), but is most commonly paired with React via the `react-redux` library.",
        "mistake": "Assuming Redux is built into React. It is a completely separate third-party library created by Dan Abramov.",
        "summary": "A third-party global state management library that provides a single, predictable, centralized data store for JavaScript applications."
