        "id": 85,
        "title": "What are Actions in Redux?",
        "category": "State Management & Redux",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Actions are plain JavaScript objects that send data from your application to the Redux store. \nThey are the *only* source of information for the store. An action MUST have a `type` property (usually a string) that describes what is happening, and optionally a `payload` property containing the actual data.",
        "example": "A user submits a login form. The UI creates an Action describing the event, and sends it to the store.",
        "code": "// A standard Redux Action object\nconst loginAction = {\n    type: 'auth/LOGIN_SUCCESS',\n    payload: {\n        userId: 123,\n        username: 'alice'\n    }\n};\n\n// Dispatching it\ndispatch(loginAction);",
        "tip": "Instead of writing objects manually everywhere, developers usually write 'Action Creators'—functions that generate and return the action object for you.",
        "mistake": "Trying to pass a Promise or a Function as an action. Actions must be plain, serializable JSON objects (unless you are using middleware like Redux Thunk).",
