        "id": 86,
        "title": "What are Reducers in Redux?",
        "category": "State Management & Redux",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Reducers are pure JavaScript functions that take the current `state` and an `action` as arguments, and return a brand new `state` object. \nActions describe the *fact* that something happened, but Reducers dictate *how* the application's state changes in response.",
        "example": "The Reducer looks at the action `type: 'INCREMENT'`, takes the current state `count: 0`, and returns a new state `count: 1`.",
        "code": "// A pure reducer function\nconst initialState = { count: 0 };\n\nfunction counterReducer(state = initialState, action) {\n    switch (action.type) {\n        case 'counter/increment':\n            // MUST return a brand new object!\n            return { ...state, count: state.count + 1 };\n        case 'counter/decrement':\n            return { ...state, count: state.count - 1 };\n        default:\n            // If action is unrecognized, return current state unchanged\n            return state;\n    }\n}",
        "tip": "Because there is only one Store, all Reducers are combined into one 'Root Reducer' using `combineReducers`.",
        "mistake": "Mutating the `state` argument directly (e.g., `state.count += 1; return state;`). React-Redux relies on referential equality (memory addresses) to know if state changed. Mutating skips re-renders.",
