        "category": "State Management & Redux",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Redux Toolkit (RTK) is the official, strongly recommended toolset for writing Redux logic today. \nLegacy Redux required massive amounts of boilerplate: creating action types, action creators, massive switch statements in reducers, and manual store configuration. RTK wraps all of this in an incredibly clean API (`createSlice`, `configureStore`), drastically reducing code.",
        "example": "With `createSlice`, RTK automatically generates the Action Creators and Action Types based on the names of your reducer functions.",
        "code": "import { createSlice } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\n\n// RTK automatically creates actions based on these reducers!\nconst counterSlice = createSlice({\n    name: 'counter',\n    initialState: 0,\n    reducers: {\n        // Look! We are \"mutating\" state! RTK uses Immer.js under the hood\n        // to safely translate this into immutable updates automatically.\n        increment: (state) => state + 1, \n        decrement: (state) => state - 1\n    }\n});\n\nexport const { increment, decrement } = counterSlice.actions;\nexport default counterSlice.reducer;",
        "tip": "If you start a new React project and need Redux, you MUST use RTK. Legacy Redux (writing switch statements) is officially discouraged by the Redux team.",
        "mistake": "Writing legacy Redux switch statements in a modern 2024 interview. It shows your knowledge is vastly outdated.",
        "summary": "The modern, official standard for writing Redux. It drastically reduces boilerplate code, simplifies store configuration, and allows 'mutative' syntax using Immer.js."
