        "title": "What is Code Splitting?",
        "category": "Performance & Optimization",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Code Splitting is a feature supported by bundlers like Webpack or Vite. Instead of packing your entire application into one massive `bundle.js` file (which takes forever to download on a slow network), it splits the code into smaller, separate files (chunks). \nThese chunks are then loaded on demand (Lazy Loading).",
        "example": "An e-commerce site has an Admin Dashboard. Standard users should never have to download the Admin JavaScript. Code Splitting ensures the Admin chunk is only downloaded if someone actually navigates to the `/admin` URL.",
        "code": "// Standard Import (Included in main bundle, slows down initial load)\nimport AdminPanel from './AdminPanel';\n\n// Code Splitting Import (Creates a separate chunk, loaded on demand)\nconst AdminPanel = React.lazy(() => import('./AdminPanel'));",
        "tip": "Code splitting at the Route level is the single most effective way to improve the initial load time (Time to Interactive) of a massive SPA.",
        "mistake": "Code splitting tiny UI components (like a Button). The overhead of making a separate network request for a 2KB button is worse than just including it in the main bundle.",
        "summary": "The process of dividing the application's JavaScript bundle into smaller chunks that are dynamically loaded only when needed, drastically improving initial page load times."
