        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Throttling is a technique used to limit the maximum number of times a function can be called over time. \nUnlike Debouncing (which waits for the user to *stop*), Throttling enforces a strict heartbeat. If you throttle a function to 1000ms, it will execute exactly once per second, no matter how many times it was triggered during that second.",
        "example": "Tracking window scrolling or mouse movement. If you fire a state update on every single pixel scrolled, the app will freeze. Throttling ensures the update only fires once every 100ms while scrolling.",
        "code": "import throttle from 'lodash/throttle';\n\nfunction ScrollTracker() {\n    // Ensure this expensive calculation only runs at most once every 200ms\n    const handleScroll = useCallback(\n        throttle(() => {\n            console.log(\"Scroll position:\", window.scrollY);\n        }, 200),\n        []\n    );\n\n    useEffect(() => {\n        window.addEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);\n        return () => window.removeEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);\n    }, [handleScroll]);\n}",
        "tip": "Rule of thumb: Use **Debounce** when you care about the *final* state (e.g., finishing typing). Use **Throttle** when you care about *intermediate* states (e.g., continuous scrolling or dragging).",
