        "category": "Performance & Optimization",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Virtualization (Windowing) is a technique where you only render the exact DOM nodes that are currently visible in the browser viewport. \nIf a list has 10,000 items, but the screen can only fit 10 items at a time, Virtualization only renders 10 `<li>` tags. As the user scrolls, it reuses those same 10 DOM nodes, swapping out the data instantly. The DOM never gets larger than 10 nodes, keeping memory usage flat and scrolling butter-smooth.",
        "example": "Using libraries like `react-window` or `react-virtualized`.",
        "code": "import { FixedSizeList as List } from 'react-window';\n\nfunction FastList({ massiveDataArray }) {\n  const Row = ({ index, style }) => (\n    // 'style' absolutely positions the row to simulate scrolling\n    <div style={style}>\n      Row {index}: {massiveDataArray[index]}\n    </div>\n  );\n\n  return (\n    <List\n      height={500} // Viewport height\n      itemCount={10000} // Total items\n      itemSize={35} // Height of each row\n      width={300}\n    >\n      {Row}\n    </List>\n  );\n}",
