        "category": "Advanced & Modern React",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Hydration is the process used in Server-Side Rendering (SSR). \nThe server sends down a dry, static HTML page so the user sees the content instantly. However, that HTML is 'dead'—buttons won't click and inputs won't work. \nOnce the JavaScript bundle finishes downloading in the background, React boots up, compares the Virtual DOM to the existing server-rendered HTML, and 'hydrates' it by attaching the missing event listeners and state logic, bringing the page to life.",
        "example": "Like watering a dry sponge. The sponge (HTML) is already there taking up space, but watering it (Hydration) makes it flexible and interactive.",
        "code": "import { hydrateRoot } from 'react-dom/client';\nimport App from './App';\n\n// Instead of createRoot (which wipes the div and builds from scratch),\n// hydrateRoot tells React: \"The HTML is already there, just attach the events!\"\nhydrateRoot(document.getElementById('root'), <App />);",
        "tip": "A 'Hydration Mismatch' error occurs if the HTML generated by the server does not exactly match the HTML React expects to generate on the client (e.g., rendering a timestamp like `Date.now()`).",
