        "title": "What is Error Boundary?",
        "category": "Advanced & Modern React",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "An Error Boundary is a special React component that catches JavaScript errors anywhere in its child component tree, logs those errors, and displays a fallback UI instead of crashing the entire application (the 'White Screen of Death'). \nError Boundaries catch errors during rendering, in lifecycle methods, and in constructors.",
        "example": "If a deeply nested `<UserProfile>` component crashes because it tried to map over `null`, an Error Boundary wrapped around the `<Dashboard>` will catch it and show 'Failed to load profile', while leaving the rest of the app (like the Sidebar and Header) fully functional.",
        "code": "// Wrapping a fragile component\n<ErrorBoundary fallback={<h1>Something went wrong.</h1>}>\n    <FragileComponent />\n</ErrorBoundary>",
        "tip": "Error Boundaries DO NOT catch errors in asynchronous code (like `setTimeout`), event handlers (like `onClick`), or Server-Side Rendering.",
        "mistake": "Trying to implement an Error Boundary using Functional Components. Currently, Error Boundaries MUST be written as Class Components.",
        "summary": "A React component that catches JavaScript errors in its child component tree and displays a fallback UI instead of crashing the entire application."
