        "title": "What is Higher-Order Component (HOC)?",
        "category": "Advanced React & Architecture",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "A Higher-Order Component (HOC) is an advanced React pattern used for reusing component logic. \nIt is not an API; it is a pattern derived from functional programming. An HOC is a **function that takes a component as an argument and returns a new, enhanced component**.",
        "example": "Wrapping a component in a `withAuth` HOC. The HOC checks if the user is logged in. If they are, it renders the component. If not, it redirects them.",
        "code": "// The HOC function (usually prefixed with \"with\")\nfunction withAuth(WrappedComponent) {\n    // Returns a NEW component\n    return function EnhancedComponent(props) {\n        const isAuthenticated = checkLoginStatus();\n        \n        if (!isAuthenticated) return <p>Please login.</p>;\n        \n        // Passes props through to the original component\n        return <WrappedComponent {...props} />;\n    }\n}\n\n// Usage:\nconst ProtectedDashboard = withAuth(Dashboard);",
        "tip": "HOCs were the primary way to share logic in older Class components. Today, Custom Hooks have almost entirely replaced HOCs because Hooks don't cause 'Wrapper Hell'.",
        "mistake": "Mutating the original component inside the HOC instead of returning a new one. HOCs must be pure functions.",
        "summary": "A functional pattern where a function takes a component and returns a new component with injected props or enhanced logic."
