        "category": "Testing",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Testing in React is generally divided into three layers:\n1. **Unit Testing:** Testing individual pure functions, reducers, or custom hooks in isolation. (Usually done with Jest or Vitest).\n2. **Component/Integration Testing:** Rendering a React component in a simulated DOM, interacting with it (clicking buttons, typing), and verifying the UI output changes correctly. (Done with React Testing Library).\n3. **End-to-End (E2E) Testing:** Spinning up a real browser and testing the entire application from front to back, exactly as a user would. (Done with Cypress or Playwright).",
        "example": "Jest verifies that `2 + 2 = 4`. RTL verifies that clicking the 'Submit' button shows a 'Success' message. Cypress verifies that a user can successfully log in and buy a product against a real database.",
        "code": "// A balanced testing pyramid:\n// 10% E2E Tests (Slow, expensive, tests critical user journeys)\n// 60% Integration Tests (Tests React components working together)\n// 30% Unit Tests (Tests complex pure logic and math)",
