        "title": "What is React Testing Library?",
        "category": "Testing",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "React Testing Library (RTL) is the official recommendation for testing React components. \nIts core philosophy is: **'The more your tests resemble the way your software is used, the more confidence they can give you.'** \nInstead of testing the internal state of a component (like checking if `state.isOpen === true`), RTL forces you to test what the user actually sees (like checking if a `<div role=\"dialog\">` is visible on the screen).",
        "example": "A user doesn't care that a boolean state changed. They care that a success message appeared. RTL tests the message, not the boolean.",
        "code": "import { render, screen, fireEvent } from '@testing-library/react';\nimport Counter from './Counter';\n\ntest('increments counter on click', () => {\n    // 1. Render the component in a virtual DOM\n    render(<Counter />);\n    \n    // 2. Find the button using accessibility roles (how a user finds it!)\n    const button = screen.getByRole('button', { name: /add/i });\n    \n    // 3. Interact with it\n    fireEvent.click(button);\n    \n    // 4. Assert the visible result\n    expect(screen.getByText(/count: 1/i)).toBeInTheDocument();\n});",
