        "id": 146,
        "title": "Difference between shallow rendering and deep rendering?",
        "category": "Testing",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "These concepts originate from the older Enzyme testing library.\n- **Deep Rendering:** Renders the component AND all of its children, exactly as it would in a real browser. (This is what React Testing Library does exclusively).\n- **Shallow Rendering:** Renders ONLY the specific component being tested. If the component contains a `<Child />`, shallow rendering replaces the child with a dummy stub. \nReact Testing Library strongly discourages shallow rendering because it creates false confidence (the parent might pass but the child might crash in reality).",
        "example": "If `<Dashboard>` renders `<Chart />` and `<Chart />` has a fatal bug. Shallow rendering `<Dashboard>` will pass the test. Deep rendering will fail (which is what you want).",
        "code": "// Shallow (Enzyme) - Stubs out children\nconst wrapper = shallow(<Dashboard />); \n\n// Deep (React Testing Library) - Renders everything\nconst { container } = render(<Dashboard />);",
        "tip": "If asked this in an interview, explain the difference, but immediately state that modern best practices (React Testing Library) explicitly avoid shallow rendering in favor of integration testing.",
        "mistake": "Trying to do shallow rendering in React Testing Library. It is intentionally omitted from the library.",
        "summary": "Shallow rendering only renders a parent component while stubbing out its children, whereas deep rendering renders the entire component tree as it would appear in the browser."
