        "title": "What is snapshot testing?",
        "category": "Testing",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Snapshot testing is a Jest feature that captures the exact rendered output (usually the HTML string) of a component and saves it to a text file. \nOn future test runs, Jest generates a new snapshot and compares it to the saved file. If even a single character of HTML has changed, the test fails, alerting the developer of an unexpected UI change. If the change was intentional, the developer presses a button to update the saved snapshot.",
        "example": "Ensuring that a highly critical `<Button>` component never accidentally changes its CSS classes or HTML structure due to a typo in a refactor.",
        "code": "import renderer from 'react-test-renderer';\nimport Button from './Button';\n\ntest('Button renders correctly', () => {\n  const tree = renderer.create(<Button label=\"Click\" />).toJSON();\n  \n  // Jest will create a .snap file on the first run.\n  // On subsequent runs, it will compare the output to that file.\n  expect(tree).toMatchSnapshot();\n});",
        "tip": "Snapshot tests are very brittle. They fail if you add a simple `<div>` wrapper. Because of this, many modern teams have moved away from them in favor of visual regression testing (tools like Chromatic or Percy).",
        "mistake": "Mindlessly pressing 'Update Snapshot' every time a test fails without actually looking at the diff to see what broke.",
        "summary": "A testing technique that saves the HTML output of a component to a file and compares it against future test runs to detect unexpected UI mutations."
