        "id": 153,
        "title": "Why should you avoid massive components?",
        "category": "Enterprise Architecture & Best Practices",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Massive components (components with hundreds of lines of logic, state, and UI) violate the Single Responsibility Principle. \nThey are extremely difficult to test, impossible to reuse, and prone to massive performance issues. If a tiny piece of state changes in a massive component, the entire 1,000-line DOM tree is forced to re-render.",
        "example": "A `Dashboard` component that fetches data, formats dates, handles user authentication, and renders a sidebar, header, and 5 charts.",
