        "id": 154,
        "title": "What is the container/presentational pattern?",
        "category": "Enterprise Architecture & Best Practices",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "A classic React architectural pattern that separates logic from UI. \n- **Container Components:** Handle the 'logic' (fetching data, managing state, talking to Redux). They render no UI themselves, only Presentational components.\n- **Presentational Components:** Handle the 'look' (HTML/CSS). They have no idea where the data comes from; they just receive it via props and render it.",
        "example": "A `UserListContainer` fetches users from the API. It passes the array to a `UserList` presentational component to map out the `<li>` elements.",
        "code": "// Container (Logic)\nfunction UserProfileContainer({ id }) {\n    const { data, loading } = useQuery(['user', id], fetchUser);\n    \n    if (loading) return <Spinner />;\n    // Passes data to UI\n    return <UserProfile data={data} />; \n}\n\n// Presentational (UI)\nfunction UserProfile({ data }) {\n    return <h1>{data.name}</h1>;\n}",
        "tip": "Since the introduction of Custom Hooks, the strict Container/Presentational pattern is used less frequently, as Hooks elegantly extract logic without needing wrapper components.",
        "mistake": "Over-engineering simple components. Not every button needs a container.",
