        "category": "Performance & Best Practices",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Dark mode requires three components:\n1. **Global State:** A Context provider or Zustand store holding a boolean (`isDarkMode`).\n2. **Persistence:** Saving that preference in `localStorage` so it survives a page refresh.\n3. **CSS Variables:** Instead of hardcoding colors, use CSS Custom Properties (`--bg-color`). When dark mode is active, you attach a `.dark` class to the `<body>` tag, which flips all the CSS variables.",
        "example": "If using Tailwind CSS, you enable `darkMode: 'class'` in the config, and simply wrap your app in a component that toggles the `dark` class on the HTML tag.",
        "code": "// 1. The React Logic\nuseEffect(() => {\n    if (isDarkMode) {\n        document.documentElement.classList.add('dark');\n        localStorage.setItem('theme', 'dark');\n    } else {\n        document.documentElement.classList.remove('dark');\n        localStorage.setItem('theme', 'light');\n    }\n}, [isDarkMode]);\n\n/* 2. The CSS Variables */\n:root { --bg: #ffffff; --text: #000000; }\n.dark { --bg: #121212; --text: #ffffff; }\n\nbody { background: var(--bg); color: var(--text); }",
