        "id": 175,
        "title": "What is Immer.js?",
        "category": "Performance & Best Practices",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Immer is a tiny, brilliant library that completely solves the pain of immutable state updates in React. \nIt provides a `produce` function. Inside this function, it gives you a 'draft' copy of your state. You are allowed to write standard, mutating JavaScript (like `.push()` or `.name = 'Bob'`) directly on this draft. \nBehind the scenes, Immer intercepts your mutations using JS Proxies, calculates exactly what changed, and safely returns a brand new, perfectly immutable object for React.",
        "example": "Redux Toolkit (RTK) includes Immer out of the box, which is why modern Redux allows you to mutate state directly in reducers.",
        "code": "import { produce } from 'immer';\n\nconst [user, setUser] = useState({ name: 'Alice', address: { zip: 10001 } });\n\n// Using Immer (Looks like mutation, but perfectly safe!)\nconst updateZip = () => {\n    setUser(\n        produce(draft => {\n            // No spread operators! Just direct assignment.\n            draft.address.zip = 99999;\n        })\n    );\n};",
        "tip": "Mentioning Immer in an interview when asked about 'complex state updates' proves you have worked in modern, production-grade codebases.",
        "mistake": "Trying to return the draft object from the `produce` function. You just mutate the draft; you don't return it.",
        "summary": "A library that allows developers to write code that appears to mutate state directly, while secretly using Proxies under the hood to generate perfectly immutable state updates for React."
