        "title": "What is Babel?",
        "category": "React Ecosystem & Tooling",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Babel is a JavaScript compiler (specifically, a transpiler). \nIt takes modern, next-generation JavaScript (ES6+) and JSX syntax, and compiles it down into backwards-compatible ES5 JavaScript that can run in any older browser. \nBrowsers have no idea what `<div>` or `<App />` means in a JavaScript file. Babel turns that JSX into `React.createElement()`.",
        "example": "Babel transforms Arrow Functions, Optional Chaining, and JSX into standard Javascript.",
        "code": "// Developer writes (JSX + ES6):\nconst App = () => <div className=\"box\">Hello</div>;\n\n// Babel compiles it to (Browser readable ES5):\nvar App = function App() {\n  return React.createElement(\n    \"div\",\n    { className: \"box\" },\n    \"Hello\"\n  );\n};",
        "tip": "While Babel was king for years, faster compilers written in lower-level languages like Go (esbuild) and Rust (SWC) are rapidly replacing it in tools like Vite and Next.js.",
