        "title": "What is a PWA (Progressive Web App)?",
        "category": "React Ecosystem & Tooling",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "A Progressive Web App (PWA) is a standard web application (like a React SPA) that uses modern browser APIs to provide a native mobile app experience. \nPWAs can be installed directly onto a user's phone home screen (without going through the App Store), they can work entirely offline, they can send Push Notifications, and they run in a standalone window without the browser URL bar.",
        "example": "Twitter (X) and Starbucks have famous PWAs. If you visit them on mobile Android, you are prompted to 'Add to Home Screen'. Once added, they look and act exactly like native apps.",
        "code": "// A PWA requires two main things:\n// 1. A manifest.json (Provides the App Icon, Name, and Theme Colors)\n// 2. A Service Worker (Provides offline caching and push notifications)\n\n// manifest.json example:\n{\n  \"short_name\": \"MyReactApp\",\n  \"name\": \"My Awesome React PWA\",\n  \"start_url\": \".\",\n  \"display\": \"standalone\",\n  \"theme_color\": \"#000000\"\n}",
        "tip": "PWAs are highly favored by businesses because they bypass the Apple/Google App Store 30% revenue tax, as the transaction happens on the open web.",
        "mistake": "Assuming PWAs work perfectly on Apple devices. Apple (iOS) heavily restricts PWAs (poor Push Notification support, strict storage limits) to protect their App Store monopoly.",
