        "title": "What is WebSockets?",
        "category": "React Ecosystem & Tooling",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "WebSockets are an advanced communication protocol that provides a full-duplex, continuous, two-way connection between the browser (React) and the Server. \nUnlike standard HTTP (where the client must constantly 'ask' the server if there is new data), a WebSocket stays permanently open. The server can push new data to React at any time, instantly.",
        "example": "Used for live chat applications (Discord/Slack), live stock market tickers, multiplayer browser games, or real-time collaborative editing (Google Docs).",
        "code": "// Standard HTTP (Client must initiate):\n// React: \"Any new messages?\" -> Server: \"No\"\n// React: \"Any new messages?\" -> Server: \"Yes, here is one.\"\n\n// WebSocket (Server pushes data instantly):\n// React & Server shake hands once.\n// Server: \"Here is a message.\"\n// Server: \"Here is another message.\"",
        "tip": "Socket.io is the most popular library for implementing WebSockets in React and Node because it automatically falls back to HTTP long-polling if the user's firewall blocks WebSocket connections.",
        "mistake": "Using WebSockets for basic CRUD operations (like updating a user profile). WebSockets are heavily stateful and consume significant server resources. Use standard REST/GraphQL APIs unless real-time pushing is strictly required.",
        "summary": "A communication protocol that establishes a persistent, two-way connection between the React client and the server, enabling real-time, instantaneous data transfer without polling."
