    {
        "id": 197,
        "title": "How do you use WebSockets in React?",
        "category": "React Ecosystem & Tooling",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "In React, WebSockets are almost always established inside a `useEffect` hook to ensure the connection opens when the component mounts. \nCrucially, you MUST return a cleanup function from the `useEffect` to close the socket connection when the component unmounts, otherwise you will create massive memory and network leaks.",
        "example": "A live chat component using the native browser WebSocket API.",
        "code": "import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\n\nfunction LiveChat() {\n    const [messages, setMessages] = useState([]);\n\n    useEffect(() => {\n        // 1. Open the connection\n        const socket = new WebSocket('wss://api.example.com/chat');\n\n        // 2. Listen for server pushes\n        socket.onmessage = (event) => {\n            const newMsg = JSON.parse(event.data);\n            setMessages(prev => [...prev, newMsg]);\n        };\n\n        // 3. CLEANUP: Close connection on unmount!\n        return () => socket.close();\n    }, []); // Empty array ensures it only connects once\n\n    return <div>{messages.map(m => <p>{m.text}</p>)}</div>;\n}",
        "tip": "For enterprise apps, wrap the socket connection in a custom React Context `<SocketProvider>` high up in the tree so multiple components can share a single open connection.",
        "mistake": "Instantiating the `new WebSocket()` outside of the `useEffect`, directly in the render body. This will create a brand new WebSocket connection to your server every time the user types a keystroke.",
        "summary": "By initializing the connection inside a useEffect hook, attaching event listeners to update state when data arrives, and rigorously closing the connection in the useEffect cleanup function."
    },
    {
        "id": 198,
        "title": "What is GraphQL?",
        "category": "React Ecosystem & Tooling",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "GraphQL is a query language and runtime for APIs developed by Facebook. \nIt was created to solve the massive inefficiencies of REST APIs. \nIn REST, a server defines multiple endpoints (`/users`, `/posts`). In GraphQL, there is only ONE endpoint (`/graphql`). The React frontend sends a query exactly describing the shape of the data it wants, and the server returns exactly that data—nothing more, nothing less.",
        "example": "Solving the 'Over-fetching' problem. If a React component only needs a user's `name` to render an Avatar, a REST API might return a massive JSON object with their address, email, and history. GraphQL allows React to request *only* the `name`.",
        "code": "// A GraphQL Query sent from React\nquery GetUserProfile {\n  user(id: \"123\") {\n    name\n    avatarUrl\n    // Notice we did NOT ask for email or address!\n  }\n}\n\n// The exact JSON response\n{\n  \"data\": {\n    \"user\": {\n      \"name\": \"Alice\",\n      \"avatarUrl\": \"https://...\"\n    }\n  }\n}",
        "tip": "GraphQL also solves 'Under-fetching' (the N+1 problem). Instead of making 1 request for a user, and 5 separate requests for their 5 posts, GraphQL can fetch the user AND their posts in a single, deeply nested query.",
        "mistake": "Assuming GraphQL is a database technology (like SQL). It is not. It is an API layer that sits *in front* of your database (which could be Postgres, MongoDB, or even other REST APIs).",
        "summary": "An API query language where the frontend explicitly requests the exact shape and fields of data it needs from a single endpoint, eliminating over-fetching and under-fetching."
    },
    {
        "id": 199,
        "title": "How do you use GraphQL in React?",
        "category": "React Ecosystem & Tooling",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Technically, you can use the native `fetch` API to send a POST request with your GraphQL string. However, enterprise React apps almost exclusively use specialized GraphQL clients like **Apollo Client** or **URQL**. \nThese clients provide custom React hooks (`useQuery`, `useMutation`) that automatically handle caching, loading states, error handling, and normalizing the complex nested data that GraphQL returns.",
        "example": "Using Apollo Client to fetch data inside a component.",
        "code": "import { useQuery, gql } from '@apollo/client';\n\n// 1. Define the query\nconst GET_USER = gql`\n  query GetUser {\n    user(id: \"1\") { name }\n  }\n`;\n\nfunction Profile() {\n  // 2. The Hook executes the query and handles state automatically\n  const { loading, error, data } = useQuery(GET_USER);\n\n  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;\n  if (error) return <p>Error : {error.message}</p>;\n  return <h1>{data.user.name}</h1>;\n}",
        "tip": "GraphQL heavily relies on Code Generation (GraphQL CodeGen). Because the API has a strict schema, you can run a script that automatically generates TypeScript interfaces for every single query in your React app.",
        "mistake": "Trying to manage GraphQL responses in Redux. GraphQL clients have their own incredibly sophisticated cache mechanisms (like Apollo Cache). Mixing them with Redux leads to duplicate data and nightmare synchronization issues.",
        "summary": "By utilizing specialized libraries like Apollo Client or URQL, which provide React hooks to execute queries, manage loading states, and automatically cache the results."
    },
    {
        "id": 200,
        "title": "What is Apollo Client?",
        "category": "React Ecosystem & Tooling",
        "language": "jsx",
        "explanation": "Apollo Client is the industry-standard, most comprehensive state management library for managing GraphQL data in React. \nIt acts as the intermediary between your React components and the GraphQL server. \nIts most powerful feature is its normalized, in-memory cache. If you fetch a list of Tasks, Apollo stores them by their unique ID. If you later run a mutation to update Task #5, Apollo instantly updates that specific task in the cache, and React automatically re-renders any component on the screen that was displaying Task #5.",
        "example": "If React Query is the king of REST APIs, Apollo Client is the king of GraphQL APIs.",
        "code": "import { ApolloClient, InMemoryCache, ApolloProvider } from '@apollo/client';\n\n// 1. Initialize the Client and its powerful Cache\nconst client = new ApolloClient({\n  uri: 'https://api.example.com/graphql',\n  cache: new InMemoryCache() \n});\n\n// 2. Wrap the React App\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    <ApolloProvider client={client}>\n      <Dashboard />\n    </ApolloProvider>\n  );\n}",
        "tip": "Apollo allows you to update the UI instantly (Optimistic UI) before the server even responds. If the server later returns an error, Apollo automatically rolls the UI back.",
        "mistake": "Assuming Apollo is just for data fetching. It is actually a complete global state management solution, effectively replacing the need for Redux in GraphQL-heavy applications.",
        "summary": "A robust state management library and data-fetching client specifically built for GraphQL, featuring powerful normalized caching, hooks, and optimistic UI capabilities."
    }
